-- CK-67: a CLOSED wrapper is comparable whatever its payload's methods
-- infer: `W` holds an `H.Holder T`, `H.eq` needs `T`'s `key`, and `W`'s
-- derived context is empty however `key` is typed (checker-v2.md §11.2).
-- `Main` is round 2's `s1n` (`key` unannotated); `Nested` is `s1n2`, where
-- `key`'s own body compares `W`s, so `key` is in flight when `W`'s context
-- is asked for. In both, `same 1 1` is `True` and `same 1 2` is `False`:
-- expected `True`, `False`, `True`, `False`.
--
-- Oracle twin, run on 7427828: this project with both `key`s annotated
-- `T, () -> Int` prints exactly the expected four lines. As written,
-- 7427828 refuses both comparisons with NOT EQUATABLE `W`: the capability
-- walk decides before `key`'s scheme exists.
pub type Holder a
    = Holder a


pub eq : Holder a, Holder a -> Bool
    where a.key : a, () -> Int
eq left right =
    case left of
        Holder l ->
            case right of
                Holder r ->
                    l.key () == r.key ()
